# Wireless Technologies & Security

---

# Wireless Networking

Wireless networks use radio frequencies to allow devices to communicate without physical cables.

---

# DFS — Dynamic Frequency Selection

- Automatically changes Wi-Fi channels when interference is detected  
- Commonly used in 5 GHz networks  

## Purpose
- Avoid radar interference  
- Improve wireless performance  

---

# TPC — Transmit Power Control

- Automatically adjusts wireless signal strength  

## Benefits
- Reduces interference  
- Improves battery life  
- Optimizes coverage  

---

# Wireless Network Types

---

## IBSS — Independent Basic Service Set
- Also called **Ad Hoc Mode**  
- Devices communicate directly without an access point  

### Example
- Laptop-to-laptop wireless connection  

---

## BSSID — Basic Service Set Identifier
- MAC address of a wireless access point  

### Purpose
- Identifies a specific wireless network device  

---

## ESSID — Extended Service Set Identifier
- Wireless network name (SSID) shared across multiple access points  

### Purpose
- Allows roaming between access points  

---

# Captive Portal

- Web page shown before internet access is granted  

## Common Uses
- Hotels  
- Airports  
- Public Wi-Fi  

---

# Wireless Authentication

Verifies the identity of devices/users before allowing network access.

## Methods
- Password authentication  
- Enterprise authentication servers  
- Certificates  

---

# Access Control List (ACL)

Controls which devices are allowed or denied access.

## Example
- MAC address filtering  

---

# Wireless Security

---

# WPA — Wi-Fi Protected Access
- Improved security over WEP  
- Uses encryption for wireless traffic  

---

# WEP — Wired Equivalent Privacy
- Older wireless encryption method  
- Weak and insecure  
- **Should not be used**  

---

# WPA2
- Stronger security standard  
- Uses AES encryption with **CCMP**

## CCMP
- Counter Mode Cipher Block Chaining Message Authentication Code Protocol  
- Provides:
  - Encryption  
  - Integrity  
  - Authentication  

---

# WPA3
- Latest wireless security standard  
- Stronger protection against attacks  

## Uses GCMP
- Galois/Counter Mode Protocol  
- Faster and more secure encryption  

---

# WPA Modes

---

## WPA/WPA2/WPA3 Personal
- Uses a shared password (Pre-Shared Key / PSK)  

### Common Use
- Home networks  

---

## WPA/WPA2/WPA3 Enterprise
- Uses authentication server (RADIUS)  

### Features
- Individual user authentication  
- Better security  

### Common Use
- Business environments  

---

# Wireless Antennas

---

## Omnidirectional Antenna
- Sends signal in all directions  

### Example
- Home Wi-Fi routers  

---

## Directional Antenna
- Focuses signal in one direction  

### Benefits
- Longer range  
- Stronger focused signal  

---

# Wireless LAN Controller (WLC)

Centralized device used to manage multiple wireless access points.

## Functions
- Configuration management  
- Security policies  
- Roaming support  

---

# Wireless Network Modes

---

## Wireless Mesh
- Multiple access points connect wirelessly to each other  

### Benefits
- Large coverage area  
- Self-healing network  

---

## Ad Hoc Mode
- Direct device-to-device communication  
- No access point required  

---

## Point-to-Point
- Direct wireless link between two locations/devices  

### Example
- Building-to-building connection  

---

## Infrastructure Mode
- Devices connect through an access point  

### Most common wireless mode  

---

# Quick Notes

- DFS changes channels automatically  
- TPC adjusts signal strength  
- WPA3 is the most secure wireless standard  
- Avoid WEP due to weak security  
- Omnidirectional antennas cover wide areas  
- Directional antennas focus signals in one direction  
- WLC centrally manages wireless networks
